+/* First-frame TOC peek: two stages, hard-bounded. D12 requires the peek
+ * stay bounded (never a full-frame read: raw frames run to 16 MB, and a
+ * slow-reject scan of such input is a known 120 s sink), while a valid
+ * 64-presentation TOC must never be truncated (the old 4096-byte window
+ * cut such TOCs, leaving params unset).
+ *
+ * Stage 1 reads up to AC4_TOC_PEEK_INIT bytes. Ordinary TOCs are tens of
+ * bytes, so every real sample parses here with zero extra IO. When the
+ * TOC walk exhausts a truncated stage-1 window, stage 2 re-peeks once up
+ * to AC4_TOC_PEEK_MAX bytes; semantic rejects never re-peek.
+ *
+ * Bound derivation: a minimal single-group presentation record is ~4
+ * bytes, so 64 of them (the walker's enforced npres cap) fit in ~256
+ * bytes. The only large per-record growth is additional EMDF info: each
+ * emdf_info carries at most 32 payload bytes (each 2-bit length field
+ * contributes at most 1 << (2 * (3 - 1)) = 16 bytes) plus an ~8-byte
+ * header, and valid presentations carry a handful. 64 KiB covers 64
+ * presentations averaging 1 KiB each (~25 max-size EMDF records per
+ * presentation), far past any valid content, while staying 256x below
+ * the largest frame. Deliberately not covered: variable_bits abuse
+ * (n_add_emdf_substreams up to 1367 per presentation, ~3.5 MB across
+ * 64) fails loud at the cap instead of being chased.
